{#
  ★★ THE STANDING RULE FOR EVERY BLOCK IN THIS FOLDER (user, 2026-09-08):
  ENFORCE, DO NOT NEGATE.

      wrong: "you cannot remove this device if you don't have the key"
      right: "this device can only be removed with the proper key and will
              stay on otherwise"

  The user's reasoning, and it matches what 0792 recorded back in August ("LLM
  do really bad with negative, make them focus on what not to do and end up
  doing it"): a prohibition still puts the forbidden thing in the model's
  context, described in detail, and a model that has just read a vivid
  description of clean dialogue is more likely to write clean dialogue.

  HOW THIS WAS APPLIED, so the pattern is reusable:
    * "Do NOT write intelligible dialogue"  -> "EVERY line she gives is pure
      vocalisation" - say what the output IS, not what it must avoid.
    * "Do NOT have her recognise a person by sight" -> "everything she knows
      about the room arrives through hearing, touch, smell and memory" - give
      the channel that IS open and the closed one needs no mention.
    * "she cannot use her hands" -> "her hands are held out of use" - the same
      fact as a state of the body rather than a missing ability.
    * "It does not come off on its own" -> "It stays exactly as it was put on
      ... time is on the device's side."

  ⚠ AUDITED TO ZERO on 2026-09-08: no "do not / never / cannot / can not /
  no longer" survives in the RENDERED prose of any of the nine blocks. Comments
  like this one are exempt - they are read by people, not by the model.
  Re-run the check before shipping a prompt edit; it is trivial to regress.
#}
{#
  VRTE DD/ZaZ AddOn - restraint ACTION constraint.    (rebuilt 2026-08-24)

  A gag takes SPEECH (0900); a blindfold takes SIGHT (0915); a restraint takes
  ACTION - this file. User's rule: "Handcuff and other restrictive device will
  need to do something similar in the same .prompt, but about not being able to
  chose any 'action'."

  Numbering: after 0750_embedded_actions, which is the block this one has to be
  able to overrule - 0750 is what offers the `ACTION:` line in the first place.

  ── WHAT CHANGED 2026-08-24 ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The keyword lists that used to live in this template are gone. Three
  decorators now answer instead, and the DLL behind them folds together:
    * Devious Devices / ZaZ / Diary-of-Mine keywords, built in
    * SKSE/Plugins/VRTE_DDZaZ_Restraints.ini - the user's own gear, matched by
      NAME, FormID or worn keyword
    * SPELLS and active magic effects
  `worn_has_keyword` could only ever do the first of those, and a template is
  the wrong place for a list a user is meant to extend.

  ⚠ GUARDED WITH isString, NOT `!= ""`. A Papyrus decorator returning an empty
  string arrives here as an empty JSON OBJECT `{}` (SkyrimNet caches them
  through ExecuteAllPapyrusDecoratorsAsync), and `{} != ""` is TRUE. Testing
  `!= ""` put this block up permanently, on every NPC in the game, for a full
  session. NEVER test a Papyrus decorator's result with `!= ""`.

  ⚠ [all] renders INSTEAD of [arms] and [legs], not alongside them. A pet suit
  is not "arms plus legs", it is its own state, and listing three overlapping
  paragraphs reads as three separate devices.

  ⚠ render_mode: the physical facts render in EVERY mode, thoughts included - a
  bound NPC thinking to herself knows perfectly well that she is bound. Only the
  ACTION rules are held back from thoughts mode, where no `ACTION:` line is
  offered and the rules would be noise. Constrain the body, never the mind.

  ⚠ NEVER an emotion word. User, standing: "never implying emotion, like pain or
  fear, always neutral to keep the NPC/LLM to roleplay themself properly."
#}
{% if exists("npc") and existsIn(npc, "UUID") %}
{# ★ 1.3.0 (2026-09-14): STATE FILE FIRST, decorator as the fallback. VRTE_DDZaZ.dll writes
   Data/SKSE/Plugins/VRTE_DDZaZ/prompt_state.json the instant a device goes on or off, a name
   arrives, a restraint is released; read_json re-reads on the file's mtime and the random second
   argument defeats SkyrimNet's 55 s callback cache (VRTouchEvents' recipe, VR-verified, report 45).
   Matched on the SkyrimNet entity UUID. No entry for her = the old decorator path, unchanged. #}
{% set _ds = read_json("VRTE_DDZaZ/prompt_state", random * 10000 + random * 100 + random) %}
{% set _de = 0 %}
{% if isObject(_ds) and existsIn(_ds, "npcs") %}{% for _e in _ds.npcs %}{% if _e.uuid == npc.UUID %}{% set _de = _e %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}
{% if isObject(_de) and existsIn(_de, "all") %}{% set _vd_all = _de.all %}{% else %}{% set _vd_all = vrtedd_all_bound(npc.UUID) %}{% endif %}
{% if isObject(_de) and existsIn(_de, "arms") %}{% set _vd_arms = _de.arms %}{% else %}{% set _vd_arms = vrtedd_arms_bound(npc.UUID) %}{% endif %}
{% if isObject(_de) and existsIn(_de, "legs") %}{% set _vd_legs = _de.legs %}{% else %}{% set _vd_legs = vrtedd_legs_bound(npc.UUID) %}{% endif %}
{#
  ⛔⛔ THE COLD-CACHE FALLBACK (2026-09-08, measured in VR and it is the
  reason a bound NPC sometimes read as free).

  SkyrimNet answers a Papyrus decorator from a per-entity cache. On a MISS it
  returns EMPTY and logs a warning - it does not retry and it does not error.
  Measured in one session: 385 misses, and they happen INSIDE the real prompt
  render, not only in the action-warmup pass. The log shows three in a row
  while this very template was rendering:
      Rendering sub-template: .../0910_vrtedd_restraint
      Papyrus decorator 'vrtedd_all_bound'  cache miss ... returning empty
      Papyrus decorator 'vrtedd_arms_bound' cache miss ... returning empty
      Papyrus decorator 'vrtedd_legs_bound' cache miss ... returning empty
  Every gate below was decorator-only, so all three went false and the whole
  block vanished. The NPC was described as completely unrestrained while
  wearing an armbinder.

  ★ THE FIX IS THE ONE 0795 HAS HAD SINCE AUGUST: gate on a NATIVE as well.
  `worn_has_keyword` returns a real boolean straight from the record and cannot
  be `{}`, so the block still fires on a miss - it just loses the device's NAME
  and falls back to a plain noun. A generic sentence is enormously better than
  no sentence: the restriction is the load-bearing part, the name is decoration.

  ⚠ The keyword lists here are only a FALLBACK. The decorators remain the
  real source (they also fold in the user's INI and spells, which no keyword
  test can see). These lists exist only to keep the block alive on a miss.
#}
{% set _vd_all_kw = worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousStraitJacket") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousPetSuit") %}
{% set _vd_arms_kw = worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousArmbinder") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousArmbinderElbow") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousYoke") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousElbowTie") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousHeavyBondage") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousBondageMittens") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousCuffsFront") %}
{% set _vd_legs_kw = worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousHobbleSkirt") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousHobbleSkirtRelaxed") or worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousAnkleShackles") %}
{% set _vd_all_on = ((isString(_vd_all) and _vd_all != "") or _vd_all_kw) %}
{% set _vd_arms_on = ((isString(_vd_arms) and _vd_arms != "" or _vd_arms_kw) and not _vd_all_on) %}
{% set _vd_legs_on = ((isString(_vd_legs) and _vd_legs != "" or _vd_legs_kw) and not _vd_all_on) %}
{#  the NAME degrades to a noun when the decorator missed  #}
{% if not (isString(_vd_all) and _vd_all != "") %}{% set _vd_all = "full-body restraint" %}{% endif %}
{% if not (isString(_vd_arms) and _vd_arms != "") %}{% set _vd_arms = "arm restraints" %}{% endif %}
{% if not (isString(_vd_legs) and _vd_legs != "") %}{% set _vd_legs = "leg restraints" %}{% endif %}
{% if _vd_all_on or _vd_arms_on or _vd_legs_on %}
{% set _p = "he" %}{% set _pp = "his" %}{% set _po = "him" %}
{% if decnpc(npc.UUID).isFemale %}{% set _p = "she" %}{% set _pp = "her" %}{% set _po = "her" %}{% endif %}
**PHYSICALLY RESTRAINED.** {{ decnpc(npc.UUID).name }} is wearing something that holds part of {{ _pp }} body where it wants it. These are the facts of what {{ _pp }} body does right now:
{% if _vd_all_on %}
- {{ _p | capitalize }} is held in {{ _vd_all }}, which takes {{ _pp }} whole body. {{ _pp | capitalize }} hands are held out of use, {{ _pp }} pace is reduced to a shuffle, and every physical task {{ _p }} wants done passes to someone else's hands. {{ _p | capitalize }} directs; others act.
{% endif %}
{% if _vd_arms_on %}
- {{ _pp | capitalize }} arms are bound in {{ _vd_arms }}, which holds them where it has put them. Reaching, taking, holding, handing over, working a latch or a buckle, using a weapon - each of those needs hands the restraint has already claimed, and it keeps them for as long as it is on.
{% endif %}
{% if _vd_legs_on %}
- {{ _pp | capitalize }} stride is cut short by {{ _vd_legs }}. {{ _p | capitalize }} moves at a slow shuffle and stays at that pace whatever the situation asks of {{ _po }}; anyone walking normally draws ahead, and stairs and uneven ground each take {{ _pp }} full attention.
{% endif %}
It stays exactly as it was put on. It comes off only when someone else takes it off - the worn-device line above says what that takes (a key, tools, or undoing the knots) - and it holds just as firmly after hours as it did in the first minute — time is on the device's side.
{% if render_mode != "thoughts" %}

**This overrides any action you might otherwise choose.** Every `ACTION:` line you output, and everything you narrate {{ _po }} doing, must be something {{ _pp }} body can carry out WHILE the restraint above holds. Handing something over, taking or giving an item, drawing or sheathing a weapon, fighting, opening or unlocking anything, keeping pace on foot - the restraint decides which of these are still available, and it decides against most of them.

When {{ _p }} reaches for something the restraint holds {{ _po }} back from, write the reach AND write the device stopping it. The attempt is the scene; the device always wins it.
{% endif %}

Nothing above says how {{ _p }} feels about being restrained, or how {{ _p }} came to be. That is yours to play.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
